Apply database schema migrations from the backend directory before starting
the application or running the seed/data-generation scripts:

    alembic upgrade head

The application and scripts do not create tables automatically. Alembic is the
only way the schema changes. Generate a revision with:

    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the schema change"

New database
------------
`alembic upgrade head` creates the full schema (revision 001 is a real
baseline, 002 adds work-order lifecycle columns). Then run `python seed_db.py`
for demo users, the parts catalog and sample data. The seeded passwords are
demo-only: do not use them outside local development.

Existing database created before Alembic was used
-------------------------------------------------
Back up the database file first. Revision 001 keeps existing tables and data,
and adds the missing NOT NULL constraints, indexes and foreign keys on
`prediction_logs`. It refuses to continue if prediction logs reference machines
or parts that do not exist. Missing machines (typically from predictions made
for IDs that were never registered) can be registered automatically:

    alembic -x repair_orphans=true upgrade head

Orphaned parts cannot be invented and must be fixed manually. Revision 002
refuses to continue if several active work orders exist for the same machine
and part; cancel or complete the duplicates first.

Do not `alembic stamp head` on an existing database: it skips these repairs.

Notes
-----
- Downgrading revision 001 drops every application table and its data.
- SQLite foreign keys are enabled by the application on every connection;
  migrations run without them, as recommended for SQLite batch operations.
- Tests run migrations on temporary SQLite databases and compare the result
  with the ORM models. PostgreSQL has not been exercised yet; run
  `alembic upgrade head` against a disposable PostgreSQL database before relying on it.
